Q: How to embed a const char in a const string? A: <p>VisualStudio 2026 and 2022 support the T4 template (file name extension <code>.tt</code>) which is a way you can write macros and do macro processing. Basically you create a template file in your VS project, write your code in in such file and choose your output file name extension with these directives:</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;#@ template language=&quot;C#&quot; #&gt;
&lt;#@ output extension=&quot;.cs&quot; #&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Then every time the <code>.tt</code> template file is saved or modified, the related .cs file (C# source code) would be automatically generated. You can then build your solution with that.</p>
<p>So instead of defining <code>char</code> constants in your C# level code, define them in T4's initializing control block:</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;#
    const char c = 'x';
    // ... more
#&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>In the lower half of <code>.tt</code> file (which is the template part), you can reference your T4 constants with the <code>&lt;#= expression #&gt;</code> syntax, for example</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>const string s = &quot;&lt;#= c #&gt;&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p>As for &quot;<strong>I have a switch expression on strings, so they must be const, and I also have a switch expression on chars, so they must also be const</strong>&quot; and not wanting to define 2 sets of constants (of <code>char</code> and of <code>string</code>)... T4 macros solve this problem easily. Use <code>'&lt;#= c #&gt;'</code> whenever you need the char-type constant of <code>c</code> and <code>&quot;&lt;#= c #&gt;&quot;</code> whenever you need the string-type constant of <code>c</code>. I don't have your code, but the following snippet can give you an idea:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;#@ template language=&quot;C#&quot; #&gt;
&lt;#@ output extension=&quot;.cs&quot; #&gt;
&lt;#
    // Values defined at the T4 level
    const char a = 'a';
    const char b = 'b';
    // ... more
#&gt;
namespace MyNamespace
{
    public static class MyClass
    {
        public static void Foo(char c)
        {
            switch (c)
            {
                case '&lt;#= a #&gt;':
                    break;
                case '&lt;#= b #&gt;':
                    break;
            }
        }
        public static void Bar(string s)
        {
            switch (s)
            {
                case &quot;&lt;#= a #&gt;&quot;:
                    break;
                case &quot;&lt;#= b #&gt;&quot;:
                    break;
            }
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>If you copy and paste the above code to your <code>.tt</code> file, it will automatically generate the following content in another file of the same base name but with file name extension <code>.cs</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>namespace MyNamespace
{
    public static class MyClass
    {
        public static void Foo(char c)
        {
            switch (c)
            {
                case 'a':
                    break;
                case 'b':
                    break;
            }
        }
        public static void Bar(string s)
        {
            switch (s)
            {
                case &quot;a&quot;:
                    break;
                case &quot;b&quot;:
                    break;
            }
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Please refer to official T4 document here:</p>
<p><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/modeling/writing-a-t4-text-template" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/modeling/writing-a-t4-text-template</a></p>
<p><strong>Drawback of using T4</strong></p>
<p>IntelliSense won't work, refactoring won't work, syntax highlighting won't work... In fact, since VisualStudio treats the whole <code>.tt</code> file like a plain text file, it can only help you automatically trigger the T4 engine to convert the file to target <code>.cs</code> file.</p>
